KZ2 patch 2173: the Metal backend creates pipeline states through one MTLBinaryArchive.

Written for the Pyrrhus native Mac bring-up; no game code or RPCS3 code used.
Licence: MIT (project LICENSE).

A pipeline's first build pays the Metal compiler -- the MTLCompiler service
round-trip -- on the thread that is drawing. On the Mac route that thread is the
FIFO walker, and the first-seen pipelines of a level load stalled it for up to
32 s at a time (the walker sampler spent 43 s of one 120 s window inside
newRenderPipelineStateWithDescriptor; the ftrace of the same window shows an
otherwise idle machine -- GPU waits total 6.5 s, no readbacks over 13 ms). The
attribution to depth readbacks waited for the GPU does not survive that
measurement: the stall is the walker itself compiling. Metal's own per-app
cache cannot serve the sandboxed runner, whose writable world is one folder,
so every run started cold.

PS3RECOMP_METAL_PSO_ARCHIVE=<file> opens one archive for the process. Every
pipeline state now goes through eng_new_pipeline(): the archive-attached create
with MTLPipelineOptionFailOnBinaryArchiveMiss is the hit probe (a hit compiles
nothing), a miss builds exactly the way today's code does, and a successful
build's functions are recorded in the archive for the next run, serialized
every 16 recordings and at backend shutdown. An archive that fails to load
(another GPU, another OS, corrupt bytes) is deleted and started over; a
descriptor the archive cannot hold is built the plain way and not recorded.
Unset, or =0, keeps the plain build of today. The port defaults the file beside
the game's other writes; the flow is pinned by tools/runtime/test_metal_pso_archive.py.

